                title: "Spinal Curl",
                intro: "This version bends mostly through the spine, not the hips: the back curls down over the pad and uncurls back up.",
                why: "With the hips resting on the pad, rounding down and rolling back up makes the erector spinae lengthen and shorten through the rep, while the glutes and hamstrings mostly steady the hips, which move only a little.",
                mistake: "Keeping the back flat and folding at the hips, the glute and hamstring version, where the lower back works only to hold its shape.",
                correct: "Curl slowly down over the pad to a mild stretch in the back, then uncurl to a straight line, hips pressed into the pad."
            ),
            TechniqueCue(
                id: "grip",
                title: "Arm Position",
                intro: "Where the arms go sets how heavy the upper body is to lift.",
                why: "Arms crossed on the chest keep their weight closer to the pad; hands at the temples or reaching overhead carry it farther out along the body, a longer lever that makes each rep harder.",
                mistake: "Swinging the arms forward to throw the body up instead of uncurling the back.",
                correct: "Cross the arms on the chest and keep them still, moving the hands to the temples only once the reps get easy."
            ),
            TechniqueCue(
                id: "spine",
                title: "Top Position",
                intro: "The top of the rep is a straight line from head to heels, not an arch.",
                why: "Uncurling back to a straight spine finishes the rep; forcing an arch past it loads the joints at the back of the spine, and repeated, forceful arching can injure backs prone to it.",
                mistake: "Arching the lower back past the straight line at the top to squeeze out more range.",
                correct: "Uncurl until the body forms one straight line, then stop there and start the next curl."
